<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iddin-Sin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2000 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iddin-Sin was a king of Simurrum, a hill kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> foothills, around the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> period, about 2000 BCE. He and his son Zabazuna left inscriptions in Akkadian and a rock relief celebrating victories over their neighbours, which reveal a brief period of independent power after the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>'s empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iddin-sin.jpg" alt="Iddin-Sin" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iddin-Sin,_King_of_Simurrum,_c._2000_BC_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
